 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Passkeys and account security
+
+Sign in and sign up continue to use the existing remote password service. After a successful password sign-in (including immediately after signup), users can create a passkey or continue to their account. Existing password accounts are linked on their first successful sign-in; passwords and remote tokens are never stored locally.
+
+The account page at `/devtools-times/account` supports creating, listing, renaming, and removing passkeys, plus editing the display name. Sensitive changes require verification with a passkey belonging to the active account or the existing password. Verification lasts five minutes. Removing the last passkey leaves password sign-in available. Changing the display name does not change the username at the remote password service.
+
+Passkey sign-in supports an explicit button and conditional username autofill. Unsupported browsers retain password sign-in. Browser APIs are called directly with `webauthn-polyfills` loaded on the client; SimpleWebAuthn verifies signatures on the server. Challenges are session-bound, expire after five minutes, and are consumed once, including unsuccessful attempts. Registration and authentication require user verification.
+
+Deployment settings:
+
+- `WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN`: the exact public origin, such as `https://news.example.com` (no path). Defaults to Astro's configured site origin; localhost uses the request origin. Production requires HTTPS.
+- `WEBAUTHN_RP_ID`: the domain associated with the passkeys, such as `news.example.com`. Defaults to the configured origin's hostname. Configure it consistently across deployments; changing the RP ID makes earlier passkeys unavailable.
+- `AUTH_STORE_PATH`: persistent credential storage path; defaults to `.data/auth.json`. Mount durable storage and back it up. The file contains account metadata and public credentials, never private keys or passwords.
+
+The file store serializes atomic updates within **one Node process**. For multiple processes or replicas, replace it with a shared transactional database and configure shared Astro session storage. Do not put this store on ephemeral production storage. Auth endpoints enforce their own same-origin checks because the sample app disables Astro's global origin checks. Production deployments should also apply rate limiting to authentication routes at the edge.
+
+Passkey provider names and icons are display hints from a small bundled subset of the [passkey AAGUID registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids). Zero or unrecognized AAGUIDs use a generic icon and label. This metadata never affects verification or account access. Browser Signal APIs synchronize accepted credentials and display names when supported; sync failures do not block account actions.
+
+Run `pnpm test:auth` for isolated security tests using real signed assertions and temporary storage, and `pnpm build` for the production build. Neither command starts an application server.
